<section id="getting-up-and-running" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="getting-up-and-running">Getting Up and Running…</h2>
<section id="installation-and-interfaces" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="installation-and-interfaces">Installation and Interfaces</h3>
<p>There are many popular relational database management systems that run SQL queries such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite, MS SQL, and Oracle. For this class, we will use SQLite since it is free, open source, robust, and easy to install and setup (no server process configuration is needed). Unlike other RDMBS programs which are implemented as a server process in which programs have to interact with a host server, SQLite allows programs to read from and write to a database disk file. This is very useful for our purposes where we quickly want to get up and running with SQL, and get a hang of the most important concepts!</p>
<p>Most of the queries we write here are standard SQL queries, shared by all programs. However, each program implements their own additional special commands which do not perfectly carry over to other programs. We will note when things are SQLite specific, as this will be relevant if you would prefer to follow along in some other particular implementation of SQL. The main “sqlite” specific statements we will be working with are dot statements. These are commands which are preceded by a <code>.</code> and are specific to sqlite (details are available <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/cli.html">here</a>). While similar command exist in other SQL implementations, you will need to check the correspondence. If you would like to see a full list of sqlite’s help files, which can be found by simply typing <code>.help</code> without any arguments at the sqlite command line interface.</p>
<p>Please see the “Setup” section on ELE for installation. After you install SQLite (sqlite3), you can try the typing sqlite3 in your bash to see if it is working. If so, you should hopefully see something like the following:</p>
<div id="fig-erd-hr" class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center anchored">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="sqlite3.png" class="img-fluid figure-img"></p>
<figcaption class="figure-caption">Figure&nbsp;1: How this may look on your screen</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<p>You may actually wish to interact with sqlite in some other way apart than through the shell. There are <a href="https://sqlitebrowser.org/">GUIs</a> which can be used, but in what remains of this document, all commands will be written as typed in sqlite at the command line shown above, with output rendered below. In class we will examine variants of these commands on the screen.</p>
</section>
<section id="overview-of-what-we-will-explore" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="overview-of-what-we-will-explore">Overview of what we will explore</h3>
<p>In practice, I want us to explore the most common SQL commands which exist in all implementations. With these commands, you will see that you can do a remarkable amount of data retrieval and processing both within and across files. By the end of a couple of practical classes with SQL, we should have an understanding of the following commands and their usage:</p>
