<section id="a-first-table" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-first-table">A First Table</h3>
<p>Let’s start by creating a table. We will use the <code>CREATE TABLE</code> SQL command. Below, we are generally writing SQL commands in all caps, though <em>command are not sensitive to capitalisation</em>, as you can see if exploring the below commands:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb1"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb1-1"><a href="#cb1-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">CREATE</span> <span class="kw">TABLE</span> test1(TestID <span class="dt">int</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="kw">null</span> <span class="kw">primary</span> <span class="kw">key</span>,text <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">50</span>));</span>
<span id="cb1-2"><a href="#cb1-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">tables</span></span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>test1</code></pre>
<p><code>.tables</code> (or <code>.table</code>) is a sqlite dot command, and shows us that we have effectively generated a table called <code>test1</code>. If we wish to see further information on what is created in memory, we can use <code>.schema</code>:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb3"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb3-1"><a href="#cb3-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">schema</span></span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>CREATE TABLE test1(TestID int not null primary key,text varchar(50));</code></pre>
<p>Let’s have a look at our first query command. One of the most common commands is <code>SELECT</code>, below we will select everything from the table that we have just generated:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb5"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb5-1"><a href="#cb5-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>Of course, because the table is empty, nothing is displayed. Let’s remedy this by starting to incorporate some data into our table. While in practice we will generally be working with databases that are already created, it is nevertheless important to know how to build things up piece by piece for times when we may have our own data management challenges…</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb6"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb6-1"><a href="#cb6-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">INSERT</span> <span class="kw">INTO</span> test1 <span class="kw">values</span>(<span class="dv">1</span>, <span class="ot">"Hello world!"</span>);</span>
<span id="cb6-2"><a href="#cb6-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">INSERT</span> <span class="kw">INTO</span> test1 <span class="kw">values</span>(<span class="dv">2</span>, <span class="ot">"Testing a second row."</span>);</span>
<span id="cb6-3"><a href="#cb6-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">INSERT</span> <span class="kw">INTO</span> test1 <span class="kw">values</span>(<span class="dv">3</span>, <span class="ot">"Now adding a third slightly longer line..."</span>);</span>
<span id="cb6-4"><a href="#cb6-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>1|Hello world!
2|Testing a second row.
3|Now adding a third slightly longer line...</code></pre>
<p>Now, when we use <code>SELECT</code> we see all the contents of the table. The default mode for printing is to simply list outputs on the screen. We could see this by typing <code>.mode</code> and see what else we can do by typing <code>.help mode</code>. Below we can see a number of variants.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb8"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb8-1"><a href="#cb8-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">mode</span> <span class="kw">column</span></span>
<span id="cb8-2"><a href="#cb8-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.width <span class="dv">10</span> <span class="dv">60</span></span>
<span id="cb8-3"><a href="#cb8-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.headers <span class="kw">on</span></span>
<span id="cb8-4"><a href="#cb8-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>TestID      text
----------  ------------------------------------------------------------
1           Hello world!
2           Testing a second row.
3           Now adding a third slightly longer line...</code></pre>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb10"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb10-1"><a href="#cb10-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">mode</span> box</span>
<span id="cb10-2"><a href="#cb10-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>┌────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ TestID │                    text                    │
├────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 1      │ Hello world!                               │
│ 2      │ Testing a second row.                      │
│ 3      │ Now adding a third slightly longer line... │
└────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre>
<p>Above we have been generically selecting everything by using <code>*</code>. The <code>SELECT</code> command allows us to select only certain fields, as below:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb12"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb12-1"><a href="#cb12-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> text <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>text
------------------------------------------
Hello world!
Testing a second row.
Now adding a third slightly longer line...</code></pre>
<p>And also allows us to print based on particular limits of the domain:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb14"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb14-1"><a href="#cb14-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> test1 <span class="kw">WHERE</span> TestID<span class="op">=</span><span class="dv">3</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>TestID      text
----------  ------------------------------------------------------------
3           Now adding a third slightly longer line...</code></pre>
</section>
<section id="creating-a-database" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="creating-a-database">Creating a Database</h3>
<p>You may have noticed that we jumped instantly into creating tables without specifying a database. Usually, a database contains a group of (inter-related) tables, and tables exist as part of a containing database. The environment that you entered by typing <code>sqlite3</code> allows you to write queries in a temporary database, but they are not saved. If you decide to exit sqlite3 now, you will lose the work that has generated the first table. Alternatively, we can create and save a database that contains the table you created, using <code>.save</code> or <code>.backup</code> (potentially depending on the version of sqlite you are using). You only need one of these to work. We will save this below, and then exit sqlite.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb16"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb16-1"><a href="#cb16-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.save firstDB.db</span>
<span id="cb16-2"><a href="#cb16-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.exit</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>Now, if we wish to enter sqlite loading the work we have done above, we can do this by typing the following at the command line:<br>
<code>sqlite3 firstDB.db</code></p>
<p>After this, you will see that we are back in sqlite, with the contents we saved in <code>firstDB.db</code>. Alternatively, we may wish to enter with an empty database, but save our work automatically in a database. This can be done by issuing a new database name when opening sqlite:</p>
<p><code>sqlite3 MyNewDB.db</code></p>
<p>Let’s close off what we’ve done so far by exiting (<code>.quit</code> or <code>.exit</code> or Ctrl+D), and open a new empty database that we will call Teaching.db. Let’s generate the following tables, which have a 1:M relation:</p>
