<p>Now let’s start to generate these tables. Below we will generate the schema, which we can then inspect with <code>.schema</code> if we like. It is worth thinking carefully about what is happening in each line here, and exploring a little bit. For example, what would happen if the name <code>Lec_ID</code> wasn’t the same in both tables?</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb17"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb17-1"><a href="#cb17-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Create</span> <span class="kw">table</span> Lecturer(</span>
<span id="cb17-2"><a href="#cb17-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Lec_ID <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>) <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="kw">null</span> <span class="kw">primary</span> <span class="kw">key</span>,</span>
<span id="cb17-3"><a href="#cb17-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Lec_name <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>),</span>
<span id="cb17-4"><a href="#cb17-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Lec_dept <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>)</span>
<span id="cb17-5"><a href="#cb17-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>);</span>
<span id="cb17-6"><a href="#cb17-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Create</span> <span class="kw">table</span> Module(</span>
<span id="cb17-7"><a href="#cb17-7" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Mod_code <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>) <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="kw">null</span> <span class="kw">primary</span> <span class="kw">key</span>,</span>
<span id="cb17-8"><a href="#cb17-8" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Mod_name <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>),</span>
<span id="cb17-9"><a href="#cb17-9" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>Lec_ID <span class="dt">varchar</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>),</span>
<span id="cb17-10"><a href="#cb17-10" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Foreign</span> <span class="kw">key</span> (Lec_ID) <span class="kw">references</span> Lecturer (Lec_ID)</span>
<span id="cb17-11"><a href="#cb17-11" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="insert-into-a-table" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="insert-into-a-table">Insert Into a Table</h3>
<p>OK, now we have two empty tables. Let’s add some data. Note here that the constraint “Foreign key” which we added at the end has an important role. It won’t allow you to add a new value for Lec_ID in the table Module, unless this value already exists in the column Lec_ID in table Lecturer. This is why we need to insert data in table Lecturer first. Each line below will add one line into the Lecturer table:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb18"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb18-1"><a href="#cb18-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'L211'</span>, <span class="st">'Abdul'</span>, <span class="st">'Computer Science'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb18-2"><a href="#cb18-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'L555'</span>, <span class="st">'Sarah'</span>, <span class="st">'Psychology'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb18-3"><a href="#cb18-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'L179'</span>, <span class="st">'Amy'</span>, <span class="st">'Physics'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb18-4"><a href="#cb18-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'L234'</span>, <span class="st">'Lou'</span>, <span class="st">'Economics'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb18-5"><a href="#cb18-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Lecturer;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>L211|Abdul|Computer Science
L555|Sarah|Psychology
L179|Amy|Physics
L234|Lou|Economics</code></pre>
<p>Now that we have done this and confirmed that the output is as we understand, we can generate the second table. Note below when we apply SELECT how the <code>NULL</code> character is displayed:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb20"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb20-1"><a href="#cb20-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'C01'</span>, <span class="st">'Intro to CS'</span>, <span class="st">'L211'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-2"><a href="#cb20-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'C02'</span>, <span class="st">'DBMS'</span>, <span class="st">'L211'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-3"><a href="#cb20-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'C22'</span>, <span class="st">'Data Science'</span>, <span class="st">'L179'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-4"><a href="#cb20-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'C39'</span>, <span class="st">'Computer Networks'</span>, <span class="st">'L179'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-5"><a href="#cb20-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'E15'</span>, <span class="st">'Econometrics'</span>, <span class="st">'L234'</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-6"><a href="#cb20-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Insert</span> <span class="kw">into</span> Module <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'U01'</span>, <span class="st">'Dissertation'</span>, <span class="kw">NULL</span>);</span>
<span id="cb20-7"><a href="#cb20-7" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>C01|Intro to CS|L211
C02|DBMS|L211
C22|Data Science|L179
C39|Computer Networks|L179
E15|Econometrics|L234
U01|Dissertation|</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-note callout-titled">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
Note
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>We noted above that the constraint “Foreign key” which we added when we created tables does not allow you to add a new value for Lec_ID in the table Module, unless this value already exists in the column Lec_ID in table Lecturer. It also does not allow you to remove a row from Lecturer if it is associated with rows from Module. However, sqlite sometimes enables or disables enforcing these restrictions by default. To see whether the foreign key constraint is enabled, use the following command: PRAGMA foreign_keys;</p>
<p>The command returns an integer value: 1: enable, 0: disabled. If the command returns nothing, it means that your sqlite version doesn’t support foreign key constraints. Please also note that PRAGMA is specific to sqlite, and so will not exist if you are using othe SQL implementations. If the sqlite library is compiled with foreign key constraint support, the application can use the PRAGMA foreign_keys command to enable or disable foreign key constraints at runtime. To disable foreign key constraint: PRAGMA foreign_keys = OFF; To enable foreign key constraint: PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;</p>
<p>For more information, see: <a href="https://www.sqlitetutorial.net/sqlite-foreign-key">https://www.sqlitetutorial.net/sqlite-foreign-key</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="update-and-delete-data-from-a-table" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="update-and-delete-data-from-a-table">Update and Delete data from a Table</h3>
<p>Once we create a table and insert data to it, we can update some of this data in it and can even delete it. Let’s just add a new row under Lecturer:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb22"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb22-1"><a href="#cb22-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">INSERT</span> <span class="kw">INTO</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">values</span> (<span class="st">'R101'</span>, <span class="st">'Marco'</span>, <span class="st">'Social Science'</span>);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>We could examine the table to be sure that things have worked as we believe. Suppose we now realise that the department of this lecturer is in fact “Sociology” rather than “Social Science”. If we would like to update some piece of information, we can use the SQL UPDATE command:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb23"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb23-1"><a href="#cb23-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">UPDATE</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">SET</span> Lec_dept <span class="op">=</span> <span class="ot">"Sociology"</span> <span class="kw">WHERE</span> Lec_ID<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'R101'</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>Note here that we have again taken advantage of the <code>WHERE</code> clause.</p>
<p>And now finally, if for some reason we want to delete this row, we can use:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb24"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb24-1"><a href="#cb24-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>DELECT <span class="kw">FROM</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">WHERE</span> Lec_ID<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'R101'</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: in the above two lines, we used the primary key (Lec_ID) to identify the row we want to change. You can use any column, but you will have to be careful since columns that are not primary key may not be unique, and you may end up updating multiple values at the same time. It’s always safest to try to query the condition under <code>WHERE</code> using <code>SELECT</code> first to make sure that the rows you are updating are the ones you want to update.</p>
<p>Finally, you may note that all of the above code is now getting somewhat elaborate, and we may actually wish to start storing it in some file which we can execute. Fortunately, if we wish save SQL code and re-run, this is quite simple! On the ELE page, we have saved the above script as a file called createTeachingTables.sql. If you open this in a text editor, you will see that this is just the lines we have laid out above. Because we are running sqlite from the command line, another way to do all of the above is to simply redirect our SQL code into the database that we wish to create as follows:</p>
<div class="cell">
<div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb25"><pre class="sourceCode bash code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode bash"><span id="cb25-1"><a href="#cb25-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ex">sqlite3</span> Teaching.db <span class="op">&lt;</span> createTeachingTables.sql</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="select-from-a-table" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="select-from-a-table">Select from a Table</h3>
<p>By now, we have already used “SELECT” several times to print table content to the screen. This is the most basic way of using select: <code>SELECT * FROM table_name</code>. But we can do other things too… Let’s explore in more detail. Sometimes, we want to query only part of the table. This is similar to the review on Pandas we had in Week1, when we moved from printing all the data frames to printing only specific parts of it. You can specify which columns you want by including their heading in the <code>SELECT</code> statement:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb26"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb26-1"><a href="#cb26-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">mode</span> box</span>
<span id="cb26-2"><a href="#cb26-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Lec_Name, Lec_dept <span class="kw">FROM</span> Lecturer;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>┌──────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Lec_name │     Lec_dept     │
├──────────┼──────────────────┤
│ Abdul    │ Computer Science │
│ Sarah    │ Psychology       │
│ Amy      │ Physics          │
│ Lou      │ Economics        │
│ Marco    │ Social Science   │
└──────────┴──────────────────┘</code></pre>
<p>And you can specify which rows you want to access by including a where statement with a condition at the level of rows (similar to fancy indexing in Pandas):</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb28"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb28-1"><a href="#cb28-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">WHERE</span> Lec_ID<span class="op">=</span><span class="ot">"L211"</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>
┌──────────┬─────────────┬────────┐
│ Mod_code │  Mod_name   │ Lec_ID │
├──────────┼─────────────┼────────┤
│ C01      │ Intro to CS │ L211   │
│ C02      │ DBMS        │ L211   │
└──────────┴─────────────┴────────┘</code></pre>
<p>Sometimes, we may not actually want to match exactly, and for this the <code>LIKE</code> statement can be very handy! INCLUDE OUTPUT</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb30"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb30-1"><a href="#cb30-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">WHERE</span> Lec_ID <span class="kw">LIKE</span> <span class="ot">"L2%"</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>┌──────────┬──────────────┬────────┐
│ Mod_code │   Mod_name   │ Lec_ID │
├──────────┼──────────────┼────────┤
│ C01      │ Intro to CS  │ L211   │
│ C02      │ DBMS         │ L211   │
│ E15      │ Econometrics │ L234   │
└──────────┴──────────────┴────────┘</code></pre>
<p>This returns all rows that have a Lec_ID starting with ‘L2’. The symbol ‘%’ means 0 or many characters of any value.</p>
</section>
<section id="select-from-multiple-tables" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="select-from-multiple-tables">Select from Multiple Tables</h3>
<p>The real strength of SQL is when we need to query data from multiple tables at the same time. For example, suppose you want to print the names of modules along with the names of the lecturers teaching these modules next to each other. This data does not exist in the same table. You need to use JOIN to join the two tables. This is where your understanding of relational algebra from last week, and SQL JOIN from this week come handy.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb32"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb32-1"><a href="#cb32-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">ON</span> Module.Lec_ID<span class="op">=</span>Lecturer.Lec_ID;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>Intro to CS|Abdul
DBMS|Abdul
Data Science|Amy
Computer Networks|Amy
Econometrics|Lou</code></pre>
<p>If we use “aliases”, we may be able to shorten the command above a little.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb34"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb34-1"><a href="#cb34-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">AS</span> M <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">AS</span> L <span class="kw">ON</span> M.Lec_ID<span class="op">=</span>L.Lec_ID;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>Intro to CS|Abdul
DBMS|Abdul
Data Science|Amy
Computer Networks|Amy
Econometrics|Lou</code></pre>
<p>Note that when the foreign key has the same name as the primary key it is referring to (which is the case here, but not necessarily always the case), we can use <code>USING(key_name)</code> instead. In other words, when we try to join two tables where the column we are using has the same name in both tables, we can instead use <code>USING</code>.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb36"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb36-1"><a href="#cb36-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">USING</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>Suppose we want to include rows from either or both of the table Lecturer and Module; for those who do not have counterparts in the other table, we can use LEFT/RIGHT/FULL with JOIN as the following:</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb37"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb37-1"><a href="#cb37-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">LEFT</span> <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">USING</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>DBMS|Abdul
Data Science|Amy
Computer Networks|Amy
Econometrics|Lou
Dissertation|</code></pre>
<p>This will give the same rows from JOIN, in addition to all (unmatched) rows from the table Module (which is typed on the left-hand side of the keyword LEFT JOIN.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, RIGHT and FULL do not seem to work as expected in SQLite.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb39"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb39-1"><a href="#cb39-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">RIGHT</span> <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">USING</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>Error: in prepare, RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOINs are not currently supported (1)</code></pre>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb41"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb41-1"><a href="#cb41-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">FULL</span> <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">USING</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>Error: in prepare, RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOINs are not currently supported (1)</code></pre>
<p>However, with some work we can substitute for those. For example, it is easy to see how we can get the outcome for RIGHT JOIN by using LEFT JOIN and swapping the order of table names!</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb43"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb43-1"><a href="#cb43-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> Mod_name, Lec_name <span class="kw">FROM</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">LEFT</span> <span class="kw">JOIN</span> Module <span class="kw">USING</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>DBMS|Abdul
Intro to CS|Abdul
|Sarah
Computer Networks|Amy
Data Science|Amy
Econometrics|Lou
|Marco</code></pre>
<p>This gives the same outcome of JOIN, in addition to all (unmatched) rows from the table Lecturer.</p>
<p>The FULL JOIN is a little tricker, but it is doable thanks to the UNION keyword (which works as the union in relational algebra): FULL is basically the union of using LEFT and RIGHT. In other words, use LEFT JOIN twice.</p>
<p><strong>SELECT</strong> Mod_name, Lec_name<br>
<strong>FROM</strong> Module <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong> Lecturer <strong>USING</strong>(Lec_ID)<br>
<strong>UNION</strong><br>
<strong>SELECT</strong> Mod_name, Lec_name<br>
<strong>FROM</strong> Lecturer <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong> Module <strong>USING</strong>(Lec_ID);</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb45"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb45-1"><a href="#cb45-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">mode</span> box</span>
<span id="cb45-2"><a href="#cb45-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Select</span> Mod_name, Lec_name</span>
<span id="cb45-3"><a href="#cb45-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">From</span> Module <span class="kw">Left</span> <span class="kw">Join</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">using</span>(Lec_ID)</span>
<span id="cb45-4"><a href="#cb45-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Union</span></span>
<span id="cb45-5"><a href="#cb45-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">Select</span> Mod_name, Lec_name</span>
<span id="cb45-6"><a href="#cb45-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">From</span> Lecturer <span class="kw">Left</span> <span class="kw">join</span> Module <span class="kw">using</span>(Lec_ID);</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>┌───────────────────┬──────────┐
│     Mod_name      │ Lec_name │
├───────────────────┼──────────┤
│                   │ Marco    │
│                   │ Sarah    │
│ Computer Networks │ Amy      │
│ DBMS              │ Abdul    │
│ Data Science      │ Amy      │
│ Dissertation      │          │
│ Econometrics      │ Lou      │
│ Intro to CS       │ Abdul    │
└───────────────────┴──────────┘</code></pre>
</section>
<section id="alter-and-drop-a-table" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="alter-and-drop-a-table">Alter and Drop a Table</h3>
<p>We talked before about UPDATE TABLE and DELETE TABLE which work at the row level. On the other hand, ALTER TABLE works at the level of columns. You can alter a table for the purpose of adding a new column, dropping an existing column, or altering an existing column. You can use DROP TABLE to remove a table and its data.</p>
<p>For example, let’s add a new column to the table Module to store the department that is offering the module. Let’s also check the new table data using select, and also check the schema.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb47"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb47-1"><a href="#cb47-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">ALTER</span> <span class="kw">TABLE</span> Module <span class="kw">ADD</span> mod_dept <span class="dt">char</span>(<span class="dv">20</span>);</span>
<span id="cb47-2"><a href="#cb47-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>┌──────────┬───────────────────┬────────┬──────────┐
│ Mod_code │     Mod_name      │ Lec_ID │ mod_dept │
├──────────┼───────────────────┼────────┼──────────┤
│ C01      │ Intro to CS       │ L211   │          │
│ C02      │ DBMS              │ L211   │          │
│ C22      │ Data Science      │ L179   │          │
│ C39      │ Computer Networks │ L179   │          │
│ E15      │ Econometrics      │ L234   │          │
│ U01      │ Dissertation      │        │          │
└──────────┴───────────────────┴────────┴──────────┘</code></pre>
<p>Let’s add some values to this column. If you examine module names, you may see that the department indicator is used a first character in the module code (e.g., ‘C01’ for a CS module). This means that we can use the LIKE command with ‘%’. This makes things easier. Before making any update, let’s check first that it works as expected.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb49"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb49-1"><a href="#cb49-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">SELECT</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="kw">FROM</span> Module <span class="kw">WHERE</span> mod_code <span class="kw">LIKE</span> <span class="st">'C%'</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>C01|Intro to CS|L211|
C02|DBMS|L211|
C22|Data Science|L179|
C39|Computer Networks|L179|</code></pre>
<p>Great. It only brings rows for modules related to computer science. Let’s do the update on all these rows.</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb51"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb51-1"><a href="#cb51-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="kw">UPDATE</span> Module <span class="kw">SET</span> mod_dept <span class="op">=</span> <span class="ot">"Computer Science"</span> <span class="kw">WHERE</span> mod_code <span class="kw">LIKE</span> <span class="st">'C%'</span>;</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<p>We can use ALTER TABLE to change the data type of any column. We have to be careful because it already contains data. For example, changing a column that contains text to INT will be a problem.</p>
