<section id="some-more-advanced-details-the-imbd-movie-database" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="some-more-advanced-details-the-imbd-movie-database">Some More Advanced Details: The IMBD Movie Database</h2>
<p>We will work now work with a slightly more elaborate example of a database with multiple related tables with thousands of rows. Before we get into this here, we will discuss the layout of this dataset and how it will be set up in relational format. In particular, we will work with the IMDB dataset file, which you may (?) have come across in your work in the prerequisite for this subject (BEE1038). As we will layout in our discussion of these data, the schema of the database that we are working with will look like this:</p>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="IMDB.png" class="img-fluid figure-img"></p>
<figcaption class="figure-caption">The IMDB Schema</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<p>In working with this database, we will have a few goals:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get a feel for working with slightly larger and more realistic data</li>
<li>See how we work with sql code in practice to replicate analyses and run commands</li>
<li>Have a look at some more advanced SQL functions such as GROUP BY</li>
</ul>
<p>To start, we will create a new database which consists of the 8 tables above (along with their constraints and relationships), and then add data to them. The data will actually be pre-generated in 8 csv files which were created from the full dataset above, and these csv files, along with a script containing SQL files is contained in a zipped folder called IMDB.zip. Let’s just examine the contents of this unzipped folder on my machine (using the command line):</p>
<div class="cell">
<div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb52"><pre class="sourceCode bash code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode bash"><span id="cb52-1"><a href="#cb52-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="bu">cd</span> ~/BEE2041/topic3/IMDB/</span>
<span id="cb52-2"><a href="#cb52-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="fu">ls</span></span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-stdout">
<pre><code>actor_df.csv
createIMDBtables.sql
director_df.csv
genre_df.csv
IMDB_example.zip
keyword_df.csv
movie_actor_df.csv
movie_df.csv
movie_genre_df.csv
movie_keyword_df.csv
orig</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Here, we can see that there are 8 tables in line with the Schema we have discussed, and what we actually want to do is to now generated our SQL database. Apart from the 8 csv files, the other thing that is contained in this folder is a file called <code>createIMDBtables.sql</code>. Let’s have a look at this (again, for the time being, simply using shell commands):</p>
<div class="cell">
<div class="sourceCode cell-code" id="cb54"><pre class="sourceCode bash code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode bash"><span id="cb54-1"><a href="#cb54-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="fu">head</span> <span class="at">-n</span> 20 createIMDBtables.sql</span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
<div class="cell-output cell-output-stdout">
<pre><code>.mode column
.width 40 40
.header ON
.separator ','

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Movie_Actor;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Movie_Genre;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Movie_Keyword;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Movie;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Director;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Actor;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Genre;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Keyword;

CREATE TABLE Director(
director_ID VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL    PRIMARY KEY,
director_name   VARCHAR(20),
director_facebook_likes INT
);</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We can see that this contains SQL commands… If we look through this file (and let’s do so!), we will see that the file contains the commands to create all 8 tables and insert all data in them. Let’s open up an sqlite3 session with a database name, for example by typing <code>sqlite3 myIMBD</code> at the command line. From within that, we can directly run code!</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb56"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb56-1"><a href="#cb56-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">read</span> createIMDBtables.sql</span>
<span id="cb56-2"><a href="#cb56-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">tables</span></span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>Actor          Genre          Movie          Movie_Genre
Director       Keyword        Movie_Actor    Movie_Keyword</code></pre>
<p>As you can see this creates the 8 tables in a database that we have named myIMDB. We can also check the schema which is similar to the content of the file createIMDBtables.sql (this is only the first few lines):</p>
<div class="interactive-sql">
<div class="sourceCode" id="cb58"><pre class="sourceCode sql interactive code-with-copy"><code class="sourceCode sql"><span id="cb58-1"><a href="#cb58-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>.<span class="kw">schema</span></span></code><button title="Copy to Clipboard" class="code-copy-button"><i class="bi"></i></button></pre></div>
</div>
<pre><code>CREATE TABLE Director(
director_ID  VARCHAR(20)    NOT NULL    PRIMARY KEY,
director_name           VARCHAR(20),
director_facebook_likes INT
);
CREATE TABLE Actor(
